{"purpose":"Record that version identifiers disagree across the standard corpus, repository READMEs, module files, and the skill-pack manifest, and state a single-source-of-truth acceptance criterion.","content":"## 1. Contradictions found\nThe same review found four independent version disagreements.\n\n**Standard corpus versus its own specification.** `specification/overview.md` opens with \"This specification is the versioned snapshot of the EKA Standard 1.1\" and later states `### Version` / `1.1.`. `specification/exchange.md` repeats \"Standard corpus version: 1.1 (this specification)\". Meanwhile `EKAv2` declares `Version 1.3`, `versioning.md` says \"snapshot of the EKA Standard 1.3 — an immutable normative specification\", and `CHANGELOG.md` documents 1.3 as current. Because `specification/` is declared authoritative on any conflict, the authoritative corpus contradicts itself.\n\n**CLI README versus module graph.** `eka-cli/README.md` states \"CLI v1.10.0 - EKA Standard 1.1 - eka-core v1.7.0\". `go.mod` requires `eka-core v1.10.5`. The embedded standard vendor reports 1.3.\n\n**Skill pack versus MCP release.** `eka-mcp/skills/manifest.yaml` declares `requiresEka: \"\u003e= 1.16.0\"` while the shipping MCP release is 1.15.1, so the pack advertises a requirement the accompanying release does not satisfy.\n\n## 2. Root cause\nThe embedded standard version is generated and asserted (`standardembed.MustVersion()` plus a shape test), so *that* axis cannot drift. Every other version statement is hand-written prose or an independent literal, with no generated manifest tying them together.\n\n## 3. Acceptance criteria\n- `specification/overview.md` and `specification/exchange.md` state 1.3, matching `versioning.md`, `EKAv2`, and the changelog.\n- A single generated manifest records the CLI version, eka-core version, standard corpus version, RSF serialization version, and skill-pack requirement, and is consumed rather than restated by README prose.\n- The skill-pack `requiresEka` value is derived from the shipping release, so a manifest can never require a version the release does not provide.\n- A test fails when any recorded version axis disagrees with its source of truth.","appliesTo":["cmd/version.go","cmd/update.go"]}